Sure! To compare the declaration flags of a parameter (`parameterObject`) and a variable declaration (`variableDeclarationObject`) you can use the `areDeclarationFlagsIdentical` helper.  
Just pass the **stringified** versions of the two declaration objects to the function, and it will return `true` if all relevant flags (optionality, privacy, protection level, async, abstract, readonly, static, etc.) match, otherwise `false`.

```ts
// Example usage – replace the JSON strings below with your actual declaration objects
const parameterObject = {
  name: "foo",
  type: "string",
  optional: true,
  visibility: "public",
  isAsync: false,
  isAbstract: false,
  isReadonly: false,
  isStatic: false
};

const variableDeclarationObject = {
  name: "bar",
  type: "string",
  optional: true,
  visibility: "public",
  isAsync: false,
  isAbstract: false,
  isReadonly: false,
  isStatic: false
};

// Convert to JSON strings (or any string representation you prefer)
const left  = JSON.stringify(parameterObject);
const right = JSON.stringify(variableDeclarationObject);

// Call the comparison function
const identical = areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({ left, right });

console.log(`Declaration flags are identical: ${identical}`);
```

If you already have the objects in your code, just stringify them (or pass them directly if the function accepts objects) and run the comparison. Let me know if you need help adapting this to your specific environment or if you’d like to see the function’s implementation!
